.TH PTRWATCH 1
.SH NAME
ptrwatch \- print pointer events for a bounded time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ptrwatch
[
.B -t
.I secs
]
[
.B -n
.I events
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ptrwatch
reads
.B /dev/pointer
(see
.IR pointer (3))
and prints each event, stopping after
.I events
of them or
.I secs
seconds, whichever comes first.
The defaults are 20 events and 10 seconds.
It then prints one of four verdict lines:
.TP
.B PTRWATCH-OK \fIn\fP event(s) in \fIs\fP s from /dev/pointer
events arrived.
The count is the evidence; a verdict without one could not be told from
a check that looked at nothing.
.TP
.B PTRWATCH-NONE no events in \fIs\fP s
the queue stayed empty.
With a pointing device attached, that means nobody moved it.
.TP
.B PTRWATCH-INUSE /dev/pointer: \fIerror\fP
.IR pointer (3)
allows one reader and refused this one.
Something else holds the slot \(em on the Pi 3B that is
.I mousecheck
in the board's init, while it is still inside its window, or a window
manager.
This is reported as its own outcome because the right next step is to
find out who, not to conclude the device is dead.
.TP
.B PTRWATCH-FAIL /dev/pointer: \fIerror\fP
the file could not be opened for any other reason.
.PP
.B PTRWATCH-READFAIL
is printed if the read returns an error or end of file part way through,
which is neither a timeout nor a success: a reader that died would
otherwise look exactly like a device that was never moved.
.SH EXAMPLE
Watch for eight seconds, from the host, through
.BR tools/jtag/boardrun :
.IP
.EX
sh tools/jtag/boardrun 'ptrwatch -t 8'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ptrwatch.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR pointer (3),
.IR usb (3),
.IR usbd (8)
.SH BUGS
It is bounded on purpose and cannot be told to wait indefinitely.
.B tools/jtag/boardrun
runs one command and waits for it, so a
.B cat
of
.B /dev/pointer
wedges the board's remote-command poller \(em and a poller that never
returns is a board that answers nothing afterwards.
.PP
.B "WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE,"
stated because on the Pi 3B it is half of a two-part measurement.
A
.B PTRWATCH-OK
says a decoded event reached
.B /dev/pointer ,
which is the whole path: controller, split transaction,
.IR usbd 's
HID decode, and the device.
A
.B PTRWATCH-NONE
says only that none arrived, and does not distinguish a device that
reported nothing from a report lost on the way.
The other half is
.B periodicdata
in
.B os/inferno/raspi3b/usbdwc.c ,
read by
.B tools/jtag/splittrace ,
which counts complete-splits that carried data at the
.I controller
\(em so
.B PTRWATCH-NONE
with a nonzero
.B periodicdata
locates the loss between the controller and
.B /dev/pointer ,
and with a zero one says the device never answered.
See
.B doc/usb-split-plan.ms .
